A||||6||prof bobo||ISO: Frame rust repair/paint advice.||||1||
Z||000000||prof bobo||04-20-2001||02:01 PM||||Now that the weather's warmin' up and I can work on my project car again, I'm trying to figure out how to deal with frame rust.  I have 4 problem areas:  Front bumper mount area, minor surface rust.  Area around windshield, mostly surface rust, but some holes eaten through on driver's side.  Passenger side rear wheel well, lots of surface rust, some rust-through in rear but doesn't seem to affect structural integrity.  Remainder is mostly surface rust in various places around sheet metal screw holes.  <P>I've been removing paint and rust with wire wheel on my angle grinder until I get to bare metal.  I assume I can just bend new sheet metal to fit the rusted out sections and weld it in place.  Not sure what to do about rust I can't get to in the boxed frame areas or where two pieces of sheet metal are sandwiched together.  Any advice/ideas?<P>Also, what's the best paint for this application?<P>Finally, is there a primer on the metal before the black paint coat?  Seems like no matter how far out I remove paint, there's a rust-colored layer of, something -I hope it's primer!||||reg||1
Z||000001||skitime||04-20-2001||02:22 PM||||Wow that sound like a lot of work! Good luck. Whenever I work with rust, I remove as much as possible to bear metal then treat with a good rust converter. I would try to get the rust converter sprayed into the areas you can't reach.||||reg||1
Z||000002||prof bobo||04-20-2001||02:46 PM||||As the original owner of this 85 GT, I've come to expect that the phrases 'Fiero' and 'lots of hard work' go hand in hand.  The hardest work has been stripping the frame bare to get access.  What keeps me going now is the fact that the _ONLY_ things I can do from this point on are improvements and putting things back together.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bubbajoexx||04-20-2001||09:39 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Dcp_0017_1.jpg"> <BR>this is rust have to replace the upper fram rails now with a tube chassis||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||04-20-2001||11:51 PM||||Maybe not the cleanest solution, but definitely an excellent catch-all for stopping the progress of rust: "Rust Check" or "Krown" brand rust inhibiting oils. These product really are incredibly effective at preventing rust where it hasn't already started and stopping it's progress where it has. 4 aerosol cans are more than enough to do a complete Fiero. The product is a bit messy, as dirt does stick to it, but it can easliy be washed off of visible areas with some engine degreaser adn water, while it remains to protect hidden areas. Highly recommended, and no, I don't sell the stuff, I'm just tired of seeing otherwise excellent cars that have been ruined because the original owner didn't know about these excellent products.||||reg||1
Z||000005||prof bobo||04-23-2001||01:39 PM||||OUCH!  Mine's not nearly that bad, looks was I was lucky and have caught it in time before it got too bad.  Is the rear passenger side a  problem area in particular?<P>I may look into the rust inhibitor oil stuff. <P>Any ideas for a permanent exterior coating better than paint would be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||04-23-2001||02:47 PM||||With as much rust damage as with bubbajoexx's, the only real solution is to cut the rusted parts out and reweld a new rear end to the car, which means you'd need a donor space age frame.  The value of the finished car should be equivelent to the cost of the work involved. Unless that is an 84 Pace Car that car isn't worth the repair cost.||||reg||3
